      "@id": "golem:G17_Character_Feature",
      "@type": "owl:Class",
      "owl:disjointWith": {
        "@id": "golem:G18_Textual_Feature"
      },
      "rdfs:comment": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Character traits or features are stable aspects defining a character's identity, including biographical (e.g., birth, death), physical (e.g., hair color, scars), and psychological features (e.g., personality). These three subcategories are specified using ecrm:E55_Type."
      },
      "rdfs:label": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "G17 Character Feature"
      },
      "rdfs:subClassOf": {
        "@id": "golem:G2_Feature"
      },
      "skos:example": "Harry Potter’s bravery as inferred from the sentence\n“Harry screwed up his courage” (chapter 17 of Harry Potter and the Philosopher’s Stone)."
    },
    {
      "@id": "dlp_extDnS:setting",
      "@type": "owl:ObjectProperty",
      "rdfs:comment": [
        "In GOLEM, links a narrative setting (G12) to various elements such as location (G13), time, character (G1), object (G16), or event (G5).",
        "The relation between a situation and the entities that constitute it. (At least some of, or all) such entities must be classified by concepts defined by the description that the situation is supposed to satisfy ([DOLCE-Lite-Plus, 2005](https://www.w3.org/2001/sw/BestPractices/WNET/DLP3941_daml.html))."
      ],
      "rdfs:label": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "setting"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "dlp_extDnS:satisfied-by",
      "@type": "owl:ObjectProperty",
      "rdfs:label": {
